.TH CKSUM 1 "2026" "spicule" "spicule User Commands"
.SH NAME
cksum \- write a cyclic redundancy check checksum and byte count for a file
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cksum
.RI [ file .\|.\|.]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B cksum
computes and writes to standard output the checksum and byte count of
each
.I file
operand, or of standard input if no
.I file
operand is given; a lone operand of
.B \-
also means standard input. Each operand is checksummed independently
and produces its own output line; if reading one operand fails,
.B cksum
still goes on to checksum the rest.
.PP
The checksum is a 32\-bit cyclic redundancy check, computed over the
concatenation of the file's own bytes followed by the file's length
encoded one byte at a time, least-significant byte first (a
zero-length file contributes no length bytes at all), and then
complemented. This is the CRC used by the historical
.B cksum
utility; it is a different CRC-32 variant (different bit ordering and
different initialization) from the CRC-32 used by gzip-style or ZIP
archive checksums, and will not agree with them for the same input. A
zero-length input always checksums to 4294967295.
.PP
For a named
.I file
operand, the output line has the form
.RS
.nf
checksum length pathname
.fi
.RE
For standard input (no operand given, or a lone
.BR \- ),
the pathname column is omitted:
.RS
.nf
checksum length
.fi
.RE
.SH OPERANDS
.TP
.I file
The pathname of a file to checksum, or
.B \-
for standard input.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Every operand was checksummed successfully.
.TP
.B 1
At least one operand could not be opened or read; the remaining
operands are still checksummed.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR dd (1),
.BR wc (1)
